# one way to input the data in AMPL format 
# for indexed parameters, the indexes are given before the value 

# Note that we have one constraint, therefore the valuer of m is one
param m := 1 ;
# Note that there are two variables, therefore the value of n is two 
param n := 2 ; 
# Note that the coefficient matrix a is size mxn, where m is the number of constraints (1) and n is the number of variables (2)
# size(a) = 1 x 2 
# One say to provide values to arrays or vectors in AMPL format is to give the index and the value 
# by providing 1 2 4, we cause the model.a[1,2] to get the value 4
# by this logic, you only need to provide one index to provide the value of a parameter only indexed by one parameter 

param a := 
 1 1 3
 1 2 4
 ;

# Note that the parameter c is only indexed by one parameter

param c := 
 1 2
 2 3
 ;

# Note that this is very different from linear algebra 
# 1 1 3 refers to constraint 1, variable 1, and this is because we declared a as model.a = pyo.Param(model.I, model.J), where I is the number of constraints, and J the number of variables 
# 1 2 4 refers to constraint 1, variable 2
# 1 2 refers to variable 1 gets the value 2 
# 2 3 refers to variable 2 gets the value 3 
# This works because we declared c as model.c = pyo.Param(model.J), where J is the number of variables 

# Naturally, since B is only indexed by m, the number of constraints, it only requires one index to fully define it
# Recall that b was defined as pyo.Param(model.I)
param b :=
 1 1
 ;